---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Crumbs from '../components/Crumbs.astro';
import { SITE } from '../lib/data';
import { webPageLd } from '../lib/ld';

const desc = "How SawChainSize.com sources and verifies chainsaw chain specs: source hierarchy, the bar-first rule, how conflicts are shown, prices and corrections.";
const jsonld = [webPageLd("How We Verify Specs: Editorial Policy", "/how-we-verify/", desc, SITE.dataDate, "WebPage")];
---
<Base title={"How We Verify Specs: Editorial Policy"} description={desc} path="/how-we-verify/" nav="about" crumbs={[{ name: 'How we verify specs', path: '/how-we-verify/' }]} jsonld={jsonld} modified={SITE.dataDate}>
  <div class="wrap">
  <div class="pagehead"><Crumbs crumbs={[{ name: 'How we verify specs', path: '/how-we-verify/' }]} /><h1>How we verify specs</h1></div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Every number on this site follows the same rule: <strong>if we cannot point to where it came from, it does not get printed as a fact.</strong> This page is the method.</p>
    <h2>Sources, in order of authority</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Manufacturer operator manuals and bar &amp; chain combination tables</strong>, e.g. Husqvarna's manual tables listing bar length, pitch, gauge, chain type and drive links per model; Echo's operator manuals naming the factory-fitted Oregon chain type.</li>
      <li><strong>Manufacturer fit-up charts and selector data</strong>, e.g. Oregon's published selection/fit-up data mapping model and bar to pitch, gauge, links and file size.</li>
      <li><strong>Chain-maker and dealer product data</strong>, e.g. Oregon product pages stating pitch, gauge, drive links and file size per part number; Stihl dealer listings stating the OEM chain designation (such as 61 PMM3 55, part 3610 005 0055) and its fitments.</li>
      <li><strong>Owner and forum evidence</strong>, used only to illustrate (for example, a real bar stamp reading) or to flag a question, never as the sole source for a published spec.</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>The bar-first rule</h2>
    <p>A model name does not determine a chain; the fitted bar does. Every spec on this site is therefore keyed to a bar length, presented with its variants separated, and paired with the bar-stamp verification step. Where the same model shipped with more than one gauge or pitch across production years, the page says so.</p>
    <p>The <a href="/tools/replacement-chain-bar-matcher/">replacement chain and bar matcher</a>, <a href="/tools/find-my-chain/">Find my chain</a> and the <a href="/tools/chain-compatibility/">compatibility checker</a> read these same rows. They do not add a spec that is not already on a saw page, and they do not turn a conflict or a verify row into a matched part number. A cross-brand part number appears only when the row already stores it, and the citation stays the source printed on that row.</p>
    <h2>When sources conflict</h2>
    <p>We print the conflict. The <a href="/saws/echo-cs-400/">Echo CS-400</a> page, for example, shows both drive-link counts for the 16″ bar with their sources, because Oregon's chain data fits a 56-link loop while Echo's own catalog lists 57. Silently picking one would make the page tidier and the reader worse off. The resolution we recommend is always the same: read the stamp on your bar.</p>
    <p>File sizes get the same treatment. STIHL’s 2025 catalog lists a 13/64″ (5.2 mm) file for its 3/8″ chain, while Oregon’s filing chart gives 7/32″ (5.5 mm) for its 3/8″ chain. On STIHL 3/8″ rows, such as the <a href="/saws/stihl-ms-362/">MS 362</a>, the file column shows both sizes with their publishers under a “File sizes differ” flag, and the <a href="/guides/stihl-chain-file-size/">STIHL file size chart</a> explains the difference. The same flag covers .325″, where STIHL lists both 11/64″ and 3/16″ and Oregon lists 3/16″, and .043″ PICCO on the <a href="/saws/stihl-ms-170-180/">MS 170 / MS 180</a>, where STIHL lists 5/32″ and Oregon lists 4.5 mm for 90PX. The flag links to that chart.</p>
    <h2>What “verify on your bar stamp” means</h2>
    <p>Some bar-length variants and older models have no specification we could trace to an authoritative source. Those cells are marked “verify on your bar stamp” rather than filled from a secondary roundup. It is a deliberate gap, and it is how you can tell the filled cells earned their place.</p>
    <h2>Prices</h2>
    <p>We show no prices. Retailer prices change daily; the price at checkout is the real one, and we never type one in by hand.</p>
    <h2>Corrections policy</h2>
    <p>If a spec is wrong, we want to know. Send the model, the bar length, and (ideally) a photo or transcription of your bar stamp via the <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>. Verified corrections are applied to the page, and where a correction changes a published spec, the page notes the change. We do not quietly edit numbers.</p>
    <h2>Retailer links and editorial independence</h2>
    <p>Buying links are added after a spec is settled, and only to items that match the spec or purpose on the page. Links to Amazon are affiliate links (the site participates in the Amazon Associates Program and earns from qualifying purchases; no other program is used). Where we opened the Amazon product page and the title matched the spec, the link goes to that product; otherwise it is an Amazon search by pitch, gauge and drive links. Bars where sources conflict get no spec-matched link. See the <a href="/affiliate-disclosure/">Affiliate Disclosure</a>.</p>
    <p class="small muted">Last reviewed: 4 November 2026.</p>
  </div>

  </div>
</Base>
